\title{financial: Financial-Structure Extensions for Merger Simulation}
\description{
Two disjoint financial-structure merger model families built on top of
the \code{antitrust} structural-fit contract:
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item A \strong{debt / limited-liability} module (migrated from \code{MergerDebt}):
firm-level debt reshapes conditional demand shares through an
outside-share default threshold.
\item A \strong{bank liquidity / stability} module (migrated from the \code{stability}
analysis project): an endogenous marginal-cost shifter driven by
liquidity stress and a pooled post-merger liquidity distribution.
}

\code{financial} depends only on \code{antitrust}. It does not depend on, or share
code with, any other sibling package. It ships its own constructors
(\code{debt_fit()}, \code{bank_fit()}) and its own small model registry rather than
reusing antitrust's internal \code{calibrate()}/\code{specify()} registry.

Choose a task guide with \code{vignette("debt-default-merger", package = "financial")}
or \code{vignette("bank-liquidity-merger", package = "financial")} in a source
bundle built with vignettes. The repository README links readable source guides
when an installation omits rendered vignettes.

Debt inputs are product-row payment contributions summed by owner. Bank inputs
use supplied point estimates or draws; no Stan toolchain is needed for numeric
vector/matrix/data.frame inputs. Both families support single-step \code{simulate()};
\code{simulate_steps()} and \code{respecify()} are unsupported.
}
